From e0a6b39a59d1b68b556068b5c181b04e1ed64a17 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Manikanta Mylavarapu <mmanikan@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Tue, 26 May 2026 08:50:46 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: save and restore Root Port config space during slot
 reset

When a PCIe slot reset is performed via the host bridge's reset_slot()
callback, the Root Port's config space can be lost during the reset
operation. This includes critical configuration such as BARs, interrupt
line assignments, and capability registers. Currently, only the endpoint
device's state is saved by the caller before reset, while the Root
Port's state is not preserved, leading to configuration loss after link
recovery. This manifests as missing legacy interrupt assignments
(PCI_INTERRUPT_LINE = 0xFF) and cleared BAR addresses after the reset
completes.

Save the Root Port's config space before invoking the host bridge's
reset_slot() callback, and restore it on success. This ensures the Root
Port maintains its configuration across slot reset operations, similar
to how endpoint device state is preserved by the caller. This is
particularly important for systems where legacy INTx interrupts are used
alongside MSI/MSI-X, Root Port BARs are configured for memory-mapped
registers, or link recovery needs to maintain pre-reset configuration.

Change-Id: I99ccf0cc4943a71ee15c79e5f74ff80f42ecc546
Signed-off-by: Manikanta Mylavarapu <mmanikan@qti.qualcomm.com>
---
 drivers/pci/pci.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/pci/pci.c b/drivers/pci/pci.c
index 9f248465acf6..73ef9aa90904 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/pci.c
@@ -5257,9 +5257,18 @@ void __weak pcibios_reset_secondary_bus(struct pci_dev *dev)
 	int ret;
 
 	if (host->reset_slot) {
+		/*
+		 * Save the config space of the Root Port before doing the
+		 * reset, since the state could be lost. The Endpoint state
+		 * should've been saved by the caller.
+		 */
+		pci_save_state(dev);
 		ret = host->reset_slot(host, dev);
 		if (ret)
 			pci_err(dev, "failed to reset slot: %d\n", ret);
+		else
+			/* Now restore it on success */
+			pci_restore_state(dev);
 		return;
 	}
 
